WEBVTT

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Priya: Okay, we're recording. This is the Northwind pilot check-in for the week of March eleventh.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Priya: Three things on the agenda: the vendor contract, the September ship date, and headcount.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Marcus: Before we start, can we add the data retention question? Legal pinged me this morning.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Priya: Sure, we'll take it at the end if there's time.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Priya: Dana, where did we land on the vendor quote?

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Dana: It came in at forty-two thousand for the year, which is two over what finance approved.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Dana: The overage is entirely the support tier. Their standard tier is business hours only.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Marcus: Business hours in which timezone? Because if it's Central European that's useless to us.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Dana: Central European, yes. That's exactly why I pushed for the upgraded tier.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Priya: So the question is whether we eat two thousand or accept overnight gaps in support.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Marcus: During a pilot I'd accept the gaps. We have maybe forty users, all internal.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Dana: I'd agree for the pilot. I would not agree for general availability.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Priya: Let's do that. Standard tier for the pilot, revisit before GA. Dana, can you go back to them?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Dana: Yes. I'll have the revised quote by Thursday and the signature packet Friday.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Priya: Who signs now that Ellis has left? That's genuinely unclear to me.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Marcus: I think it defaults to Ellis's manager, which is Rahim, but nobody has confirmed that.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Priya: Dana, add that to your Thursday note. We need a name, not a guess.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Dana: Noted.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Priya: Second item. The September ship date. Marcus, you had concerns.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Marcus: I do. The integration work is about three weeks of effort and we have four weeks of calendar.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Marcus: That's fine if nothing goes wrong. Something always goes wrong.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Dana: What's the failure mode you're most worried about?

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Marcus: The auth handoff. Their SSO is SAML only and ours has been OIDC since the migration.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Marcus: We either build a shim or we ask them to support OIDC, and they've said no once already.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Priya: How long is the shim?

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Marcus: Five days if it's clean. Two weeks if we hit token refresh problems, which I expect we will.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Priya: So worst case we're at four weeks of work in four weeks of calendar with no slack.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Marcus: Correct. And that assumes I'm not pulled onto the incident rotation, which I am, in April.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Dana: Could we ship the pilot without SSO? Local accounts for forty internal users?

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Marcus: Technically yes. Security would have to sign off on an exception.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Priya: That feels like the pressure valve. Marcus, can you scope the local-accounts path this week?

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Marcus: I can have a one-pager by Wednesday with the security exception we'd need.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Priya: Good. Decision for today: we hold the September date and we keep pilot scope frozen.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Priya: Anything that isn't in the scope doc goes to the October release. No exceptions this time.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Dana: Agreed. Last quarter we let three things in and that's why we're here.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Priya: Third item, headcount. Finance came back on the second engineer.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Priya: It's deferred to January. Not denied, deferred, and that distinction apparently matters to them.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Marcus: It matters to them. It doesn't change anything for me between now and September.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Priya: I know. I pushed. The answer was that the pilot has to show usage numbers first.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Dana: What usage numbers? Has anyone written down the threshold?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Priya: No, and that's a problem. I'll ask for a specific number and bring it back next week.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Marcus: Please do. Otherwise we'll hit September and be told the numbers weren't good enough.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Priya: Fair. I'll get it in writing.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Priya: We have four minutes. Marcus, the retention question.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Marcus: Legal wants to know how long we keep the transcripts the pilot generates.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Marcus: Right now the answer is forever, because nobody set a policy and the default is forever.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Dana: That's going to be a problem with the works council too, not just legal.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Priya: What's a defensible number?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Marcus: Ninety days for raw audio, a year for the derived summaries, is what our peers do.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Priya: Write that up as a proposal and I'll take it to legal. Can you do that by end of month?

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Marcus: Yes. It's a short document.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Priya: Okay. To recap: standard support tier for the pilot, September holds, scope is frozen.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Priya: Dana has the revised quote and the signer name by Thursday.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Priya: Marcus has the local-accounts one-pager Wednesday and the retention proposal by month end.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.000
Priya: I'm chasing finance for a written usage threshold. Same time next week.
